---
title: Home
layout: base.njk
---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">~/.agents, organized</p>
    <h1>Tallmadge</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
      A package manager and environment switcher for AI coding agents —
      skills, plugins, MCP servers, and marketplaces, all built around the
      standard <code>~/.agents/</code> directory.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ site.repo }}#installation">Install</a>
      <a class="btn" href="{{ site.repo }}">Read the docs</a>
    </div>
    <pre class="install-snippet"><code>brew tap kplawver/tap
brew install tallmadge</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="section-kicker">What it does</p>
    <h2>One source of truth for every agent</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Global plugin store</h3>
        <p>Install from a local path, git repo, GitHub shorthand, marketplace catalog, or <code>.agents</code> Hub bundle. Everything lands in <code>~/.tallmadge/store</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Selective activation</h3>
        <p>Symlink exactly the skills, agents, tasks, and memories you want into the standard <code>~/.agents/</code> directories.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Composed, not clobbered</h3>
        <p><code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>mcp.json</code> are merged from every active plugin alongside your own content — no conflicts, no overwrites.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Harness bridging</h3>
        <p>Thirteen coding harnesses, one source of truth. clpr bridges the config gaps for tools that only read part of <code>~/.agents/</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Switchable profiles</h3>
        <p>Keep <code>work</code>, <code>personal</code>, and client setups as named profiles and switch the whole environment in one command.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Repo bridging</h3>
        <p><code>clpr repo link</code> carries a repo's canonical <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>.agents/</code> layout to every harness your teammates use.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt" id="harnesses">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="section-kicker">Compatibility</p>
    <h2>Thirteen harnesses, bridged</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Skills already load natively from <code>~/.agents/skills/</code>. Tallmadge
      bridges global instructions, subagents, and MCP config for the rest —
      the full matrix of what's native, bridged, or off-limits lives in the
      <a href="{{ site.repo }}#harness-support">README</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="chips">
      {% set harnesses = ["Oh My Pi", "Pi", "Cline", "Kilo Code", "Amp", "Devin", "Claude Code", "Codex", "opencode", "Zed", "Gemini CLI", "Cursor", "Copilot CLI"] %}
      {% for harness in harnesses %}
      <li class="chip">{{ harness }}</li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="section-kicker">Why the name</p>
    <h2>A network of agents, organized</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Named for Major Benjamin Tallmadge, spymaster of the Culper Ring —
      Washington's network of agents operating under aliases across enemy
      lines. Tallmadge does the same for your AI agents: a distributed
      network, coordinated from one place.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-alt" id="projects">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="section-kicker">Projects</p>
    <h2>The members of the ring</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      {% for project in projects.projects %}
      <div class="card project-card">
        <h3><a href="{{ project.repo }}">{{ project.name }}</a></h3>
        <p class="project-tagline">{{ project.tagline }}</p>
        <p>{{ project.description }}</p>
        <p class="project-install">Install: <code>{{ project.install }}</code></p>
        <ul class="tags">
          {% for tag in project.tags %}
          <li>{{ tag }}</li>
          {% endfor %}
        </ul>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
      <div class="card card-ghost">
        <h3>More soon</h3>
        <p>This hub will grow to cover related projects — plugins, marketplaces, and harness tooling.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
